WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:12.892
In the global push for renewable energy, a surprising conflict has emerged: the race for electric vehicles (EVs) is colliding with the need to preserve the world’s remaining ancient forests.
在全球推动可再生能源的浪潮中，出现了一个令人意外的冲突：发展电动汽车的竞赛，正与保护世界上仅存的原始森林的需要发生碰撞。

00:00:13.692 --> 00:00:16.632
The heart of the issue lies in the lithium-ion battery.
问题的核心在于锂离子电池。

00:00:17.432 --> 00:00:24.562
To make these batteries lighter and more efficient, manufacturers use a coating on the copper foil that holds the electricity.
为了让这些电池更轻、更高效，制造商会在承载电流的铜箔上使用一种涂层。

00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:35.984
The most effective material for this coating is a resin derived from petrochemicals, but a growing number of companies are switching to an alternative bio-based material: rubber.
这种涂层最有效的材料是一种源自石油化工产品的树脂，但越来越多的公司正在改用一种生物基替代材料：橡胶。

00:00:36.784 --> 00:00:40.564
Natural rubber is not new, but its use in high-tech batteries is.
天然橡胶并不是什么新东西，但把它用在高科技电池里却是新鲜事。

00:00:41.364 --> 00:00:45.894
This shift has created a massive new demand for the sap of the Pará rubber tree.
这一转变催生了对帕拉橡胶树汁液的巨大新需求。

00:00:46.694 --> 00:00:57.834
While rubber plantations can be sustainable, the problem is that the majority of the world’s remaining wild rubber trees are located deep within the rainforests of the Congo Basin and the Amazon.
虽然橡胶种植园可以做到可持续，但问题在于，世界上仅存的野生橡胶树大多位于刚果盆地和亚马孙雨林的深处。

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:03.164
Tapping these wild trees does not require cutting them down, which is good.
割取这些野生树木的胶液并不需要把树砍倒，这是好事。

00:01:03.964 --> 00:01:11.014
However, the sudden economic incentive to find more rubber is leading loggers and poachers deep into protected areas.
然而，突然出现的、寻找更多橡胶的经济动力，正把伐木者和偷猎者引向保护区深处。

00:01:11.814 --> 00:01:20.194
Previously, these remote forests were less attractive to exploit because the cost of extracting the timber was too high relative to the profit.
以前，这些偏远的森林不那么值得开发，因为相对于利润，采伐木材的成本太高了。

00:01:20.994 --> 00:01:25.204
But a single wild rubber tree can now generate a steady annual income.
但现在，一棵野生橡胶树就能带来稳定的年收入。

00:01:26.004 --> 00:01:28.224
This has led to a “gold rush” mentality.
这催生了一种“淘金热”心态。

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:33.574
Prospectors are building illegal roads to reach stands of wild rubber trees.
勘探者们修建非法道路，以到达成片的野生橡胶树。

00:01:34.374 --> 00:01:45.104
Once a road is built, it acts as a spine, allowing not just rubber tappers, but also industrial loggers and bushmeat hunters to penetrate previously untouched areas.
道路一旦修好，就会像脊柱一样充当主干道，不仅让割胶工人，也让工业伐木者和丛林野味猎人得以深入此前未被触及的地区。

00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:58.054
A study published in the journal Nature last month showed that for every kilometer of legal rubber-tapping road, there are an estimated three kilometers of illegal branches carved out for timber and hunting.
上个月发表在《自然》杂志上的一项研究显示，每修建一公里合法的割胶道路，估计就会有三公里为采伐木材和狩猎而开辟的非法支路。

00:01:58.854 --> 00:02:01.844
This creates a difficult paradox for the EV industry.
这给电动汽车行业带来了一个棘手的悖论。

00:02:02.644 --> 00:02:06.884
On one hand, it needs rubber to escape the petrochemical economy.
一方面，电动汽车行业需要橡胶来摆脱对石油化工经济的依赖。

00:02:07.684 --> 00:02:20.904
On the other, the demand for natural rubber is indirectly causing deforestation, which releases massive amounts of stored carbon and destroys biodiversity—the very problems that EVs are supposed to solve.
另一方面，对天然橡胶的需求正间接导致森林砍伐，而这会释放大量储存的碳、破坏生物多样性——这恰恰是电动汽车本应解决的问题。

00:02:21.704 --> 00:02:37.726
Some battery scientists are now racing to find a third option: a synthetic biopolymer that mimics rubber’s properties but is made from plant waste like corn stalks or sugarcane bagasse, which comes from crops grown on existing farmland rather than in rainforests.
一些电池科学家正争分夺秒地寻找第三种选择：一种合成生物聚合物，它模仿橡胶的特性，却由玉米秆或甘蔗渣等植物废料制成，而这些废料来自种在现有农田里而不是雨林中的作物。

00:02:38.526 --> 00:02:47.628
Until that technology is ready, the world faces a lose-lose choice: batteries that hurt the climate via oil, or batteries that hurt the climate via forests.
在这项技术成熟之前，世界面临的是一个双输的选择：要么是通过石油伤害气候的电池，要么是通过森林伤害气候的电池。

00:02:48.428 --> 00:02:51.188
The choice is not as green as consumers might assume.
这个选择并不像消费者可能以为的那样环保。
